\mt1 Psalms

\c 6

\d For the choirmaster. On stringed instruments; on the Sheminith. A psalm of David.\f + \fr 6:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq On stringed instruments; on the Sheminith \ft The superscription gives three performance directions: (1) *lamnaṣṣēaḥ* — "for the choirmaster," the same term heading Psalm 5; (2) *binginôt* — "on stringed instruments" (lyre, harp); (3) *ʿal-haššĕmînît* — "on/according to the Sheminith." The meaning of *Sheminith* is genuinely uncertain. Four scholarly proposals circulate: (a) an eight-stringed instrument (Dahood); (b) the bass octave or lower register — instruments pitched low (Hossfeld/Zenger, preferred by most commentators); (c) a known melody called "the eighth"; (d) a liturgical designation linked to the eighth day (Midrash Tehillim — creative but secondary). Augustine read it eschatologically as "the eighth day" of resurrection beyond history's seven ages, which is theologically inventive but exegetically unsupported. The honest answer: we do not know what it means. What we do know is that this psalm was performed — with specific instruments, in a specific register, by a named director. It was not private devotion. It was public liturgical art. — Psalm 6 sits at the center of an editorial cluster (Psalms 3–7) that the editors of Book I shaped as a week's worth of crisis prayers: Psalm 3 (pursuit), Psalm 4 (accusation), Psalm 5 (legal crisis), Psalm 6 (illness), Psalm 7 (legal appeal). Psalm 6 is the nadir — maximum bodily vulnerability — after which Psalm 7 can mount a formal protest because the body has survived the night.\f*\f + \fr 6:0 \fk KULTUR \fq A psalm of David \ft *Mizmôr lĕ-dāwid* — "a psalm of David" or "a psalm in the Davidic tradition." The Hebrew preposition *lĕ-* is grammatically open: by David, for David, from the Davidic collection. The text does not resolve the question of historical authorship. What the heading does resolve: this psalm belongs to the tradition of Israel's most famous sufferer-king, the one whose life included illness, betrayal, enemies who interpreted his crises as divine punishment, and the experience of divine absence. Whether David composed these words or not, the heading invites every reader into the same posture: you are allowed to pray like the king prayed — from the body, from the dark, without apology.\f*\f + \fr 6:0 \fk LEBEN \fq the first of the seven Penitential Psalms \ft In the 6th century CE, the theologian Cassiodorus grouped Psalms 6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, and 143 as the "seven penitential psalms" — prayers of repentance. The classification became enormously influential in Western Christianity: these seven were assigned to Ash Wednesday liturgies, prescribed for dying persons, and became the default lens through which this psalm was read for fifteen hundred years. The problem: Psalm 6 contains no confession of sin. Not one word. There is no acknowledgment of wrongdoing, no penitential language, no admission that the suffering is deserved. The speaker asks God not to punish — but never says "I sinned." The psalm is structurally identical to Job's protest: I am suffering; I have not done the thing you think I did. — The penitential classification has caused documented pastoral harm across centuries. Sick people, told to pray Psalm 6 as a confession, were taught that their illness was punishment — the exact theological equation the psalm itself protests against. Peter Craigie: "One must be careful not to import the theme of penitence into the psalm, for there is no confession of sin." Goldingay: "It would be wrong to read a consciousness of sin into the psalm." Hossfeld/Zenger: the speaker "is like Job — not aware of any wrongdoing." The classification was wrong. The psalm is an illness prayer, not a guilt prayer. Reading it as penitential is reading against its own grammar.\f*

\s1 The Dark Night

\b
\q1
\v 1 LORD, do not correct me in your anger.\f + \fr 6:1 \fk TEXT \fq do not correct me... do not discipline me \ft Two different Hebrew verbs with distinct registers: *tôḵîḥēnî* (from *yāḵaḥ* — to bring a legal charge, to prove wrong, to confront with evidence; used in Proverbs 3:12 of parental correction and in Job 13:3 of legal argumentation before God) and *tĕyassĕrēnî* (from *yāsar* — to discipline, to train through suffering; used in Deuteronomy for God's formational discipline of Israel). The first is forensic: "don't put me on trial." The second is pedagogical: "don't train me through this pain." Together they cover the two ways the psalmist might interpret the suffering — as punishment for a crime, or as education through suffering. The psalmist rejects both. This is not the prayer of someone who accepts that the suffering is deserved. It is the prayer of someone who says: whatever this is, it is not correction and it is not training. — The two anger-words are also a deliberate pair: *ʾaph* (nostril-flaring; the word literally means "nose/nostril" — the flared nostrils of fury) and *ḥēmâ* (burning heat; from a root meaning "to be hot"). Cold authority-anger and hot burning-anger. The psalmist is caught between two modes of divine displeasure and asks to be released from both.\f*
\q1 Do not discipline me in your rage.

\b
\q1
\v 2 Be gentle with me,\f + \fr 6:2 \fk TEXT \fq Be gentle with me \ft The Hebrew is *ḥannēnî* — an appeal to *ḥēn* (grace, favor, generosity). It is the cry of the lower-ranked to the higher-ranked: not "I deserve this" but "be generous because you are generous." The word carries no implication of merit. It is the opposite of a legal claim — it is a relational appeal. Alter: "a plea for grace," not for justice. The translation "be gentle with me" captures the intimate, physical register of the Hebrew without the churchy weight of "have mercy" or "be gracious" — both of which, in modern English, sound like liturgical formulas rather than desperate human speech. The Hebrew is desperate human speech. — The same root returns at v.9 as *tĕḥinnâ* ("plea for grace"): what the psalmist asked for in v.2 is declared heard in v.9. The circle closes.\f*\f + \fr 6:2 \fk LEBEN \fq I am falling apart \ft The Hebrew word is *ʾumlal* — variously translated "faint," "languishing," "wretched." It appears only four times in the Hebrew Bible. In 1 Samuel 2:5 and Jeremiah 15:9 it describes a woman unable to bear children — physically depleted, socially diminished, biologically emptied. Goldingay notes that this permits the psalm to be read as "the prayer of a woman who was raped" — the word carries gendered embodied vulnerability even though the speaking "I" is grammatically open. For modern readers: the word names the experience of being physically unable to continue, the point where the body says "I cannot do another day of this." Anyone who has lived through chronic illness, depression, grief that does not lift, or the aftermath of violence recognizes the register. The psalm begins from the floor.\f*
\q1 LORD — I am falling apart.
\q1 Heal me. My bones are in shock.\f + \fr 6:2 \fk KULTUR \fq my bones are in shock \ft The Hebrew is *nibhalû ʿaṣāmāy* — "my bones are *bāhal*-ed." The verb *bāhal* means to be terrified, dismayed, thrown into panic — an emotional-somatic state. In Hebrew anthropology, bones are not merely the skeleton; they are the structural core of the self, the infrastructure that holds the person upright. When the bones are in *bāhal*, the deepest architecture of the self has collapsed. It is closer to a nervous breakdown than to a broken leg. — This is the first occurrence of *bāhal* in the psalm. It will appear again in v.3 (applied to "my whole self") and then — crucially — in v.10 (applied to the enemies). The terror transfers from the innocent sufferer to the guilty watchers. The structural argument of the entire psalm is contained in this one verb's journey across the three strophes. — A textual note: a variant reading (*bālû* = "they crumble/waste away") exists and was followed by some German translations. The Masoretic Text's *nibhalû* is the more theologically interesting reading — bones that are *terrified*, not merely decaying, give the psalm its distinctive somatic psychology.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 3 My whole self\f + \fr 6:3 \fk TEXT \fq my whole self \ft The Hebrew is *napšî* — "my *nepeš*." For four hundred years, English Bibles have rendered this "my soul," following the KJV's inheritance from Tyndale. The problem: in modern English, "soul" evokes Platonic body/soul dualism — an immortal, immaterial essence trapped in a material body. The Hebrew *nepeš* means nothing of the kind. It is the whole animate being — the breathing, desiring, hungry, frightened, embodied self. Its root meaning is "throat" — the passage through which breath and food enter, the place where desire is felt. When the psalmist says "my *nepeš* is terrified," they do not mean an invisible soul is troubled while the body remains fine. They mean: everything I am, from throat to gut to bone, is in terror. The escalation from v.2 ("my bones") to v.3 ("my whole self") is an expansion: the terror has spread from the infrastructure to the totality.\f* is shaking apart.
\q1 And you, LORD — how long —\f + \fr 6:3 \fk TEXT \fq how long — \ft The Hebrew is *ʿad-mātay* — "how long?" — with no grammatical object. The sentence breaks off. This is technically called an *aposiopesis*: a rhetorical figure in which the speaker stops mid-sentence, unable or unwilling to finish. Hossfeld and Zenger: "The question is used as aposiopesis — the suppliant breaks off mid-question. Just as suddenly should God's anger break off." — Every translation that completes the sentence ("how long will you be angry?" "how long will you make me wait?") destroys the poetic and theological meaning. The incompleteness IS the meaning. The psalmist cannot finish the sentence because the experience has exceeded language. What would you even ask? How long until you come back? How long until this ends? How long until I die? The question contains all these and refuses to settle on one. — In the English-speaking world, "How long, O Lord?" carries a second resonance: the African American civil rights tradition. Howard Thurman, Martin Luther King Jr., and the entire lineage of Black church protest-prayer use this phrase as both liturgical cry and political demand. The broken question is not only personal lament; it is the prayer of communities under systemic oppression who have asked and asked and received no answer. The phrase connects the individual sick bed to the collective suffering of peoples.\f*\f + \fr 6:3 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq And you, LORD \ft The Hebrew *wĕ-ʾattâ YHWH* — "and you, LORD" — is an accusation disguised as an address. The psalmist has named the body's terror (vv.2–3a) and now turns to God with an incomplete demand. The structure implies: my bones are shaking, my whole self is shaking, and YOU — you are doing what, exactly? The silence after "how long" is God's silence returned to God as a question. This is not pious prayer. It is confrontation. The Hebrew Bible permits this. Job does it. Jeremiah does it. Habakkuk does it. The Psalter is full of it. The idea that prayer must be polite, that God cannot be accused, that faith requires only gratitude — this is a modern evangelical distortion, not a biblical one. The psalm demonstrates that furious, broken, accusatory prayer is prayer.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 4 Come back,\f + \fr 6:4 \fk TEXT \fq Come back \ft The Hebrew imperative is *šûbâ* — from the root *šûb*, "to turn, to return." This is not a polite request. It is a command addressed to God. And it implies something theologically radical: God has *left*. The psalmist does not say "turn your attention to me" or "look at me again." The verb means: you went away, and I need you to come back. — The verb *šûb* will appear one more time in the psalm — at v.10, where the enemies "turn back" (*yāšubû*) in shame. The same Hebrew root, reversed direction: in v.4 the psalmist begs God to return; in v.10 the enemies are forced to retreat. The psalm's structural argument is embedded in this single verb: the returning that was asked of God is imposed on the enemies. What the psalmist requested upward, the psalm delivers downward. — Tim Keller (*The Songs of Jesus*, 2015) names the v.8 turn as "an answering touch" — the moment when the one praying senses that they have been heard, even before circumstances change. On this reading, *šûb* in v.4 is answered not by God visibly returning but by the psalmist suddenly knowing God never left.\f*
\q1 LORD. Pull me out.
\q1 Save me — for the sake of your faithful love.\f + \fr 6:4 \fk TEXT \fq your faithful love \ft The Hebrew is *ḥasdekā* — "your *ḥesed*." This is the load-bearing theological term of the entire psalm. *Ḥesed* is not mercy (that would be *raḥămîm*), not kindness (that would be *ṭôb*), not love in the emotional sense (that would be *ʾahăbâ*). *Ḥesed* is covenant loyalty — the faithfulness one party keeps to another because they are bound in relationship. It is what you cannot walk back. It is what holds even when the other party fails. — The argument of v.4 is therefore not "save me because I deserve it" or "save me because I am good" but "save me because you made a commitment, and your character is at stake." The psalmist is binding God to God's own covenant nature. This is the Hebrew prayer tradition's most powerful move: arguing from God's own identity rather than from human merit. — C.S. Lewis, writing about the loss of his wife in *A Grief Observed* (1961), reaches for something like this: the appeal not to God's power but to God's nature — the demand that God be who God said God would be. The appeal to *ḥesed* is the appeal to character over circumstance.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 5 No one remembers you in death.\f + \fr 6:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq No one remembers you in death. In Sheol — who thanks you \ft The argument is audacious. The psalmist is telling God: if I die, you lose. Dead people don't praise. Dead people don't remember. Dead people don't add to the chorus of those who acknowledge you. Save me — because my death diminishes you. — This is not naive theology. It is a recognized prayer convention across the ancient Near East. Akkadian *šuilla* (hand-lifting) prayers employ the identical argument: the worshiper tells the deity that a dead praiser is a lost praiser, and the deity's own reputation suffers from the death. S. David Sperling (*JANES* 33, 2018) documents the parallel in detail. The psalm is using a known rhetorical form — one that trades on the assumption that God *wants* to be praised, and that the death of a praiser is a real loss in the cosmic economy. — The theology underneath is pre-resurrection. The psalmist has no hope of an afterlife in which praise continues. Death is the end of the God-relationship. Sheol is not punishment — it is simply the place where nothing happens, where God is absent, where praise cannot occur. Craigie: "We must not too quickly impose the perspectives of later theology upon the text." The urgency of the psalm depends on this: if this life is the only arena of encounter with God, then illness that threatens death threatens everything. There is no "at least in heaven..." The stakes are total.\f*\f + \fr 6:5 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq who thanks you \ft A secular reader might ask: what kind of God needs to be thanked? Is this a narcissistic deity who saves people to maintain a fan base? The question is reasonable but misunderstands the Hebrew concept of *yādâ* ("to thank/acclaim/acknowledge"). The word does not name flattery. It names witness — the public declaration that someone acted faithfully. When the psalmist says "who will *yādâ* you in Sheol?" the question is not "who will stroke your ego?" but "who will testify to your character?" The dead cannot bear witness. The living can. The psalmist is arguing: keep me alive so that your faithfulness has a witness in the world. The argument trades on the Hebrew conviction that God's reputation in the world is mediated through the testimony of those who have experienced God's *ḥesed*. A dead witness testifies to nothing.\f*
\q1 In Sheol — who thanks you.\f + \fr 6:5 \fk KULTUR \fq Sheol \ft *Šĕʾôl* in Hebrew is not hell, not purgatory, not heaven, not "the grave" (which is *qeber*). It is the realm of the dead — a shadowy underworld where consciousness persists but relationship does not. The dead in Sheol are not punished; they simply *are not active*. They do not praise, do not remember, do not encounter God. It is parallel to the Mesopotamian *kurnugû* — the "land of no return" (Akkadian *erṣet lā tāri*). — For English-speaking readers, the concept maps onto no familiar category. It is not the Dante *Inferno*. It is not the Buddhist *bardo*. It is not "nothingness" in the secular sense (because the dead still *exist* there, they just cannot *do* anything). The closest cultural analogue might be the grief-experience of a loved one with advanced dementia — present but unreachable, existing but no longer in relationship. Sheol is relational death more than biological death. — The translation leaves "Sheol" untranslated because no English word captures it. "The grave" is too physical. "The underworld" is too mythological. "Hell" is theologically wrong. "The realm of the dead" works but is wordy. The transliteration plus this note is the honest choice.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 6 Worn out from groaning.\f + \fr 6:6 \fk TEXT \fq Worn out from groaning. Every night I flood my bed. I dissolve my couch with tears \ft This is the only tricolon in the psalm. Every other unit in the poem is a bicolon (two parallel lines). Here, three lines. The extra colon marks this as the emotional and structural apex — the lowest point, the center of gravity. Hossfeld/Zenger suggest the first colon (*yāgaʿtî bĕʾanḥātî* — "I am weary with my moaning") may echo Jeremiah 45:3 (Baruch's lament: "I am weary with my groaning") — a deliberate intertextual quotation that adds a prophetic solidarity dimension. Whether original or inserted, the tricolon functions as the psalm's gravitational center. — The verbs are hyperbolic and physical: *ʾaśḥeh* (hiphil of *śḥh* — "I cause to swim, I flood") and *ʾamsseh* (hiphil of *mss* — "I cause to dissolve, I melt"). The bed is literally swimming. The couch is dissolving. These are not metaphors in the decorative sense — they are the language of a person who has been weeping so long and so hard that the physical environment seems altered. Anyone who has wept through an entire night recognizes the report: the pillow is soaked, the sheets are wet, the body's production of tears seems inexhaustible. The psalm names this without embarrassment.\f*
\q1 Every night I flood my bed.
\q1 I dissolve my couch with tears.\f + \fr 6:6 \fk LEBEN \fq Every night \ft *Bĕḵol-laylâ* — "every night." Not one night. Every night. The weeping is chronic. The psalm assumes a sustained period of suffering, not a single crisis. This is the prayer of someone who has been in the dark for a long time — weeks, months. Modern clinical language would call this major depressive episode, or the sustained grief of chronic illness, or the relentless insomnia of anxiety. The psalm does not pathologize it. The psalm puts it into prayer. — For contemporary readers in therapy-culture: the psalm does not suggest that prayer replaces treatment, or that faith should prevent the tears. It holds the tears inside the prayer. The two are not in competition. The groaning IS the prayer. The tears ARE the offering. The body's distress is not a failure of faith; it is the content of faith.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 7 My eyes are ruined from grief,\f + \fr 6:7 \fk TEXT \fq My eyes are ruined from grief, worn thin by all my enemies \ft The Hebrew has an elegant chiastic structure: *ʿāšĕšâ mik-kaʿas ʿēnî / ʿātĕqâ bĕḵol-ṣōrĕrāy* — (a) verb (b) cause (c) subject // (c') verb (b') cause. The second colon intensifies: "becomes dim" escalates to "is torn out / worn away." Alter: "My eye is worn out from vexation, is worn out because of all my foes." — The first appearance of enemies in the psalm. They arrive only at v.7b — late, after the illness has been described in full. Their role is not as the cause of the illness but as its interpreters. These are the people who watch a sick person and conclude: "God is punishing them." In English-speaking culture, the idiom "Job's comforters" names this exact dynamic — the friend who comes to comfort but whose theology makes the suffering worse by explaining it as deserved. — The psalms scholars are unanimous: these enemies are not military opponents. They are theological interpreters of suffering — people who read illness as divine verdict. The psalmist has been sick; the enemies have turned the sickness into a judgment. The psalm's final strophe (vv.8–10) dismisses them with a sudden authority that comes from knowing: God heard me, not them.\f*
\q1 worn thin by all my enemies.\f + \fr 6:7 \fk KULTUR \fq all my enemies \ft For modern English readers, "enemies" in a psalm sounds like paranoia or ancient tribalism. The cultural work of this footnote: to identify what "enemies" means in an illness psalm. — These are not people with swords. They are people with opinions. Specifically, they are people who have interpreted the psalmist's illness as proof of sin — who have looked at a sick body and said, "What did you do to deserve this?" The dynamic is alive in every hospital ward, every chronic illness support group, every therapy session where a client says, "People keep telling me I should be better by now." In American evangelical culture, the prosperity gospel is the contemporary form of this theology: if you're sick, your faith is insufficient; if you're poor, God is displeased. The psalm was written against that theology three thousand years before it was named. — C.S. Lewis, after his wife's death, records the experience exactly: "Not that I am (I think) in much danger of ceasing to believe in God. The real danger is of coming to believe such dreadful things about Him" (*A Grief Observed*, 1961). The "enemies" are those who believe dreadful things about God's relationship to the sufferer — and say so.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 8 Get away from me,\f + \fr 6:8 \fk TEXT \fq Get away from me \ft The Hebrew is *sûrû mimmennî* — a plural imperative: "Depart from me!" The tone shifts between v.7 and v.8 without transition, without explanation, without any narrative mechanism. One moment the psalmist is weeping, eyes ruined, surrounded by interpreters of suffering. The next moment the psalmist is dismissing them with the authority of someone who knows something they do not. — This is the "sudden turn" (*Stimmungsumschwung*) that has generated more scholarly discussion than any other feature of lament psalms. Three theories: (1) A cultic oracle was pronounced between vv.7 and 8 — a priest spoke assurance that is not recorded in the text (Gerstenberger, Begrich). (2) The act of prayer itself produced the shift — lament is transformative rather than merely expressive (Craigie, Brueggemann). (3) The confidence is declared proleptically — the psalmist speaks the future into being without yet experiencing it (Westermann). — Tim Keller's pastoral formulation resonates with English-speaking readers: "an answering touch" — the sense of being heard before any visible change occurs. Not an answer to the prayer, but the knowledge that the prayer has landed. In the contemplative tradition (Centering Prayer, Quaker worship, Ignatian discernment), this is recognized as a core experience: the moment when the one praying knows, without evidence, that they are not alone.\f*
\q1 all you who make trouble —
\q1 the LORD heard the sound of my weeping.\f + \fr 6:8 \fk KONTEXT \fq the LORD heard the sound of my weeping \ft The verb is *šāmaʿ* — qatal (perfect/completed aspect). "The LORD *heard*" — past tense, accomplished fact. Not "the LORD hears" (ongoing) or "the LORD will hear" (future hope). The hearing has already happened. The psalmist announces it as accomplished. — Notice what the LORD heard: not words, not a theological argument, not a correctly formulated prayer. The LORD heard *qôl biḵyî* — "the sound of my weeping." The body's sound. The non-verbal cry. The pre-linguistic grief. This is the psalm's most radical pastoral claim: that what reaches God is not the sophisticated prayer but the sound the body makes when it cannot speak. Romans 8:26 — "the Spirit intercedes with sighs too deep for words" — is the Pauline articulation of the same conviction.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 9 The LORD has heard my plea.\f + \fr 6:9 \fk TEXT \fq has heard... will hold \ft The verse contains a deliberate tense shift. The first verb — *šāmaʿ YHWH tĕḥinnātî* — is qatal (completed): "The LORD *has heard* my plea." The second — *YHWH tĕpillātî yiqqāḥ* — is yiqtol (imperfect/future): "The LORD *will take/hold* my prayer." Alter: "a miniature narrative sequence: first God hears, then as a result will accept." The hearing is declared accomplished; the full response remains future. — The pastoral weight: the psalmist knows that being heard is not the same as being fixed. The circumstances have not changed. The body may still be sick. The enemies are still present (they will be dismissed in v.10, but not destroyed). What has changed is the *knowledge* of having been heard. For anyone who has ever felt that their prayer goes into a void — that no one is listening, that the ceiling is solid — the psalm offers this: there is a stage between "no answer" and "everything resolved." It is the stage of being heard. It is enough to change the speaker's posture from supplicant to commander.\f*
\q1 The LORD will hold my prayer.

\b
\q1
\v 10 All my enemies — shamed.\f + \fr 6:10 \fk TEXT \fq shamed... shaking apart... turn back, shamed \ft Three features of this final verse require attention. — (1) *Yibbāhălû mĕʾōd* — "they will be *bāhal*-ed greatly." This is the SAME phrase used of the psalmist in v.3: *nibhālâ mĕʾōd* ("terrified greatly"). The terror has transferred. What was happening to the innocent sufferer now happens to those who watched and judged. The psalm's structural argument is complete: *bāhal* moves from the psalmist's bones (v.2) to the psalmist's whole self (v.3) to the enemies (v.10). The verb's journey across the poem IS the poem's theology. — (2) *Yāšubû* — "they will turn back." The root is *šûb* — the same verb used in v.4 when the psalmist begged God to "come back" (*šûbâ*). The returning that was asked of God is now imposed on the enemies as retreat. Same verb, reversed direction. Asked upward in desperation, delivered downward in judgment. — (3) The divine name is ABSENT from v.10. After six occurrences of "LORD" in vv.1–9, the final verse does not name God. The silence is deliberate. The psalm ends looking at the enemies, not at God. The theology has done its work and withdrawn; what remains is the social consequence.\f*\f + \fr 6:10 \fk KULTUR \fq shamed \ft *Bôš* in Hebrew is not internal guilt (psychological shame) but social disgrace — loss of public standing, exposure before the community. It is what the enemies tried to impose on the psalmist through their theological verdict ("God is punishing you, therefore you are disgraced"). The reversal is exact: the shaming they attempted is returned to them. This is not personal vindictiveness; it is the restoration of public truth. The one who was sick was not being punished; the ones who said so are exposed as wrong. — For readers shaped by contemporary shame-research (Brené Brown, etc.): the Hebrew concept is closer to "being publicly proven wrong" than to the internal experience of toxic shame. The enemies lose face. Their theological verdict is overturned. The community now knows who was right.\f*
\q1 Shaking apart.\add  All of them.\add*
\q1 They turn back, shamed in a moment.\f + \fr 6:10 \fk LEBEN \fq in a moment \ft The Hebrew *rāgaʿ* means "in an instant" — a blink, a finger-snap. The shaming is sudden. It matches the suddenness of v.8 — the same abruptness that characterized the psalmist's shift from lament to confidence now characterizes the enemies' reversal from power to disgrace. The poem ends on this word: not a resolution, not a doxology, not a praise. Just the abrupt cessation of the enemies' power. The psalm stops. — There is no "and they all lived happily ever after." No reconciliation is offered. No forgiveness is extended. The enemies are shamed and gone. The psalmist survives. That is enough. For readers who want neat endings: the psalm refuses them. For readers who have survived something — illness, abuse, the theological verdicts of people who should have known better — the refusal of a neat ending is itself the honesty. You survive. They are wrong. That is the poem.\f*
